rocSOLVER (gfx803, ROCm 10.0): WarpSize must be 64 on every wave64 ISA, not only
on gfx9xx. The upstream gate `#if defined(__GFX9__)` leaves gfx8xx, which is also
wave64, on the 32-lane branch, and the wave-partial reductions then store each
wave's result twice.

REASON
------
A gfx803 rocSOLVER exists only once this repo builds one. The
`rocsolver-builder` stage does that with `-DAMDGPU_TARGETS=gfx803`, because
upstream's default target list is `gfx900 / gfx906 / gfx908` plus newer. hipSOLVER
needs no build of its own: it carries no device code and is a host wrapper over
rocSOLVER. A rocSOLVER that is built must also be checked for device code, because
the stock 10.0 binary ships host stubs and a kernel registration table with an
empty `.hip_fatbin` (0 bytes from `objcopy --only-section=.hip_fatbin`, against
4.7 MB for our own rocBLAS build and 43 MB for `libtorch_hip`), and every
hipSOLVER-backed `torch.linalg` entry point then dies in `hipLaunchKernel` on an
unregistered kernel handle. The `rocsolver-builder` stage gates on that size, the
same way the rocBLAS stage does.

The second, separate defect is a wrong constant in the device sources.
`lib_device_helpers.hpp` defines

    #if defined(__GFX9__)
    __device__ static constexpr int WarpSize = 64;
    #else
    __device__ static constexpr int WarpSize = 32;   /* gfx803 lands here */
    #endif

while the DPP reductions further down are enabled for `__GFX8__` on purpose and
pick `is_cdna = true`, the wave64 instruction sequence, for anything that is not
GFX10, GFX11, or GFX12. So gfx803 runs wave64 reductions while `WarpSize` says 32.

The constant's consumers are the standard one-partial-per-wave shape:

    __shared__ S sval[BLOCK / WarpSize];
    reduce_wave_sum(sum);          /* afterwards every lane holds the full 64-lane sum */
    if (tid % WarpSize == 0)       /* true on lane 0 and again on lane 32 */
        sval[tid / WarpSize] = sum;/* so one wave's sum is stored twice */
    ... combine sval[0 .. BLOCK / WarpSize - 1] /* and counted twice */

`reduce_wave_sum` itself is correct: its final broadcast uses HIP's own `warpSize`,
and `row_bcast:15` and `row_bcast:31` really do exist on GCN3, which is why
`__GFX8__` belongs in the DPP guard. The fault is only in the consumers of the
constant: `rocauxiliary_larfg_*`, `rocauxiliary_larf_*`, `rocauxiliary_lange*`,
`rocauxiliary_latrd`, and the LACN2 norm and condition-estimation helpers. Those
are the Householder generate and apply path under `geqrf`, `gehrd`, and `syevd`
(QR, SVD, eigendecomposition, `lstsq`, `pinv`, `matrix_rank`) and the norm and
RCOND paths. `WarpSize` has exactly one definition site, so this one line fixes all
of them. The other `32`s in the tree are host-side `(n-1)/32+1` block-count
rounding and are unrelated.

Measured on gfx803 (RX 470, ROCm 10.0) with a standalone replica of that exact
pattern (`tools/tc-staleness/wavesize_proof.hip`: one 256-thread block, 4096
elements, hand-counted expectation), where only the constant differs:

    WarpSize=32 (upstream on gfx803): 20288.0, ratio 1.982   /* double-counted */
    WarpSize=64 (this patch)         : 10144.0, ratio 0.991  /* correct */

The 0.9 percent deficit on the correct line is float accumulation order in the
replica. The factor of two on the upstream line is the fault.

What this patch changes, and what is proven:

  * Proven and large: building rocSOLVER for gfx803 at all. With the
    `rocsolver-builder` stage's output on the card, all 13 `torch.linalg` routines
    run and match a CPU reference, worst relative error 1.0e-5 (`qr`, `svd`,
    `svdvals`, `cholesky`, `solve`, `inv`, `eigh`, `slogdet`, `matrix_rank`,
    `pinv`, `lstsq`, `triangular_solve`, `norm('nuc')`). The stock stack SIGSEGVs
    on all 13.
  * NOT yet proven to change a result: this constant. An ablation was run with two
    libraries built from the same tree, one with this patch and one without (they
    differ, md5 `c8bd09d4...` against `f4c4c5e0...`), over 39 checks: `qr`, `svd`,
    `svdvals`, `norm('nuc')`, `matrix_rank`, `cond`, `eigh`, `cholesky`, `solve`,
    `pinv`, `lstsq`, and batched `qr`, at n=128, 256, and 512, plus non-square and
    rank-deficient inputs. Every line of output is identical between the two
    libraries.

It is kept anyway, and the reason is stated plainly rather than dressed up as a
fix: on a wave64 ISA a `WarpSize` of 32 is simply the wrong number, the same
mistake this repo already corrected in torch's `C10_WARP_SIZE`
(`patches/pytorch/gfx803-c10-warp-size-wave64.patch`, where it did produce garbage)
and in Triton's `ISAFamily::GCN3`. The pattern is provably wrong in isolation
(`tools/tc-staleness/wavesize_proof.hip` double-counts at 1.982). No rocSOLVER
routine tested here reaches a consumer in a way where that shows, so treat this as
a latent defect and not as a measured correction, and look for a case that does
before claiming credit for it. Anyone chasing that should note that
`torch.linalg.cond` routes through SVD and not through rocSOLVER's LACN2 path, so
the `lange` and condition-estimation consumers need the rocSOLVER C API
(`roclange`, `rocgecond`, `rocsyrf2` families) rather than torch to be exercised.

Apply with patches/rocsolver/rocsolver-wavesize-gfx8.sh, which uses `patch -p1`
because the target is a sparse checkout of a monorepo subdirectory.

--- a/library/src/include/lib_device_helpers.hpp
+++ b/library/src/include/lib_device_helpers.hpp
@@ -35,7 +35,14 @@
 #include "lib_macros.hpp"
 #include "libcommon.hpp"
 
-#if defined(__GFX9__)
+// WAVESIZE_GFX8_WAVE64_PATCH:
+// gfx8xx (CIK/VI) is a wave64 ISA like gfx9xx, and the DPP reductions below are
+// deliberately enabled for __GFX8__ and take the wave64 (CDNA) instruction
+// sequence. Leaving this constant at 32 therefore does not under-reduce: every
+// lane already holds the full 64-lane sum, and the consumers' one-partial-per-wave
+// pattern (guard `tid % WarpSize == 0`, index `tid / WarpSize`) then stores that
+// same sum twice, so the combine loop counts it twice.
+#if defined(__GFX9__) || defined(__GFX8__)
 __device__ static constexpr int WarpSize = 64;
 #else
 __device__ static constexpr int WarpSize = 32;
